From f96f71d150126330328c8756ee69b5f0dfc210b8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Filyus <filyus@filyus.ru>
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 19:11:57 +0500
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] feat: add tokenHelper, a program that prints a registry
 token

`//<registry>/:tokenHelper=<absolute path>` names a program that prints the
credential for a registry, the setting of the same name that pnpm has. A raw
token is sent as `Bearer <token>`; output that starts with an auth scheme and
a space is used as the header value. The credential is supplied through the
`getAuthorization({ regKey, uri })` option of npm-registry-fetch, so it is sent
as a stored `_authToken` is: same proxy and certificate settings, same http:
behaviour, same redirects. npm-registry-fetch moves to ^20.1.0, the first
version with the option, and a command that makes requests fails with
ETOKENHELPER when a helper is configured and the library in use lacks it,
instead of sending every request without a credential.

The runner (lib/utils/token-helper.js, on @npmcli/promise-spawn) takes one
absolute path with no arguments and refuses anything else, and a value that is
not an existing file is never printed: the messages name the config key and say
the value is not shown. A .cmd or .bat file runs through cmd.exe, and a path
with `"`, `%` or `!` is refused, as is one in a directory longer than 258
characters. The helper starts in its own directory with stdin closed, has 60
seconds (SIGTERM, SIGKILL 2 seconds later, the process tree on Windows) and
1 MiB per stream, is read for 250 ms after it exits, and runs once per
registry and helper per process; a failure and a refusal are cached too. Its
environment lacks the npm_config_* variables and the script directories npm put
in front of PATH, and gets NODE_OPTIONS, EDITOR, NODE_ENV and COLOR as the
user's environment had them when npm started (an npm that a script started
recognises what its parent set by what the parent exported).
NPM_INTERNAL_TOKEN_HELPER_RUNNING is set for the helper, and an npm it runs
fails with ETOKENHELPER instead of running a helper. Output is trimmed, a byte
order mark removed, and anything that is not a header value refused without
quoting it. stderr is shown with the exact token replaced by `***`: as
warnings when the helper fails, at verbose when it works. A failure is an
ETOKENHELPER error that carries helperExitCode and signal itself, so npm exits
as for any of its errors.

Only the user and global config files may set it. In project config,
publishConfig, the environment and on the command line it is ignored with a
warning, and so is a user or global file when project config sets userconfig,
globalconfig or prefix (keys compared after ${VAR} expansion), or when its path
was chosen on the command line or in the environment and its real path lies
inside a project root: the project found from the current directory by the
usual walk up the tree whatever --prefix says, and localPrefix unless the
command is global (`npm ci --prefix sub`, `npm --prefix client ci` with
NPM_CONFIG_USERCONFIG at the repository root, `--userconfig ./ci.npmrc`). A child npm is told which files its
parent did not trust in npm_config_token_helper_untrusted, set only when one of
them has a helper. The unscoped, slash-less and per-package-scope forms are
ignored with a warning that names the line to write instead.

The helper wins over a stored credential for the same registry, with one
warning per registry that names what is ignored; an OIDC trusted-publishing
token wins over both. publish, whoami and get-identity count a helper as a
login; config set refuses the unscoped form and project config.
